ry camera  in front of us  we stay like a typical news anchor and  started  welcome back everyone i'm anna frautevir  and this is cnn drama newsroom  hundreds reported killed in syrian gas  attack  according to al jazeera syrian activists  claim that government has carried out a  poisonous gas attack in the rebel-held  suburbs of the capital damascus  hundreds of people including children  were dead  and then  at the end of this hunting story  was a sentence  the local coordination committees of  syria  calls it  a massacre  a massacre  for me as an english learner it was a  new advanced vocabulary word  with disturbing meaning  and if someone had told me back then  that this word would be applied to the  situation in my country  i would have never believed them  but as you know  it happened  months ago the president of the european  council charles michelle  used this word to describe what had  taken place in ukraine  and right now  everything has changed for me  everything  today i can walk peacefully here in  brussels like nothing is happening  because i don't see it  but it's not how it works  we are too interdependent and have  solidarity that doesn't let us forget  about anyone suffering anywhere in this  world  in syria  afghanistan  india  anywhere  and this solidarity is what i want to  talk about  this is a word which definition i don't  want to give  i very much doubt that there is at least  one dictionary that will fully describe  its versatility  but i'm sure i saw that word  i saw it in action  i'm also sure that you have seen it  that you have felt this solidarity  we all realize our interdependence  and that is how we are taught since  early childhood  we help each other  we work in groups during school years  we do teamwork  and this is not that we are not complete  as an individual  it is rather that together  we are stronger  the european tradition  laying behind the democratic success  is based on the simple premise  that we have staked in one another  and that what binds us together  is greater  than what drives us apart  imagine the strength and power of one  person  and what if there are hundreds thousands  of such people  there is nothing more unifying than  solidarity  you might not win war on your own as  well as you may not carry heavy bags  home  it's such different things but still  the same meaning  indeed true solidarity is proven during  that times  since that terrifying morning  millions of ukrainians  were needed to flee their country to  leave their home to leave their city  and when you need to leave the city  you're able to take only one single  suitcase with your whole life in it  so  imagine you need to pack your whole life  into this suitcase literally you have  one hour for it  what would you take  probably documents  maybe your laptop  school diploma your favorite t-shirt  book remember you have only one suitcase  your favorite cup pair of socks  this is not simple  actually i failed this challenge is when  i was evacuating with my cat in my left  hand  everything that was really important  necessary i left behind  and  this is the reality  ukrainians face  every single day  and  these days  are as dark as knight are  but then the solidarity of millions of  people around the globe  became our light  in these darkness  each ukrainian fills gratitude towards  the world community  that helps us to survive this war  and  i'm not even talking about political  issues  the solidarity of our people is more  than just resolutions and negotiations  it is visible through human kindness  and charity  i felt this when i was living kiev  the day i left  i found what real solidarity is  a stranger helped me to bring my  suitcase and gave me a live to a safer  place  i saw how customs officer was playing  with little refugee child  just to make her happy  i know how many europeans welcomed  ukrainians about their homes among them  my friends  and i know how many european  institutions do a great job like  european solidarity corpse  they provide humanitarian aid to  refugees that were forced to uproot  their lives and rebuild them across  borders  they help them they give them another  chance  to have a better life  and this is solidarity  i believe these situations might be a  good definition  by the way have you ever experienced  running to a bomb shelter  no  well i hope you never do  imagine you finish your walk day at the  office  you are tired it's late  you're having your dinner not so good  actually  and on top of that you're getting  bombarded  i can promise it's not as fun as it  sounds  to this  it's demoralizing  you put your cloth on you go downstairs  to the shelter  praying that you're survived this night  and actually one night when we were  hiding underground  there happened the guy from my  neighborhood playing guitar at 3am  just to cheer everybody up and later my  sister said happily oh we are so lucky  to have such a great lie here in the  bomb shelter let's start drawing and her  friend raise over with pencils and  notebook  and i was like wow at that moment  later i had stories from my friends  forced to move abroad but a really kind  barman somewhere in europe after  figuring out my friends were ukrainian  refugees  gave them coffee for free with hearts  drawn on the cups  a little later again i was watching  president zielinski address to the  european parliament  everyone applauded him and later  adopted a resolution supporting ukraine  here is this  free coffee with hearts  and here is the adoption of the  resolution in the european parliament  these things are on such different  levels  but they all have one matter underlying  guess which  absolutely it's it's solidarity  and you know the best policy is born of  a sense of community and unity  which is extremely useful when  addressing problems  and challenges  we must always remain kind true open and  understanding  this is exactly the spirit that modern  europe needs  countries must be as united as people of  those countries  this is a reliable guarantee of our  safety and well-being  the pandemic  worse global warming  all of this reminds us of how  interdependent we are  i believe this might be good opportunity  to restore our consensus through  cooperation solidarity and coordination  but the chances are limited  we must learn to prevent critical turns  not just to solve them  i don't know what a tragic history for  future generations to learn  let our descendants admire our victories  successes and achievements  so today  i'm voice of those  who were killed in ukrainian cities  who were lost forever  in the infinite silence  i know for sure that those were people  who loved their lives  and who loved freedom  and i know for sure  that they want us to be joint and  several now  joint  and several  which is never something abstract  unfortunately it is now not possible to  save those people  but we are capable of saving millions of  others we are capable of making lives of  many around the globe better  and this help  starts right here  in this room  in this building  on this street  solidarity starts with you helping your  neighbor and ends somewhere there in the  high office of the u.n  there is no small solidarity  there is always  one  let us never lose  of that essential truth  and i would like to end my story with  that the anchoring thing however with  slightly different highlight  so  welcome back everyone i'm anna forza  view and this is cnn drama newsroom  hundreds reported saved  from the war in ukraine  vulnerable position in syria  or denial of rights in afghanistan  these people were able to get a new  chance to get a better life  thanks to those who do care and provided  them with the necessary assistance  so if you're lucky enough  to get up in the morning without hearing  those sirens  and the permanent threat to your  security  be grateful  and help those  who doesn't have this option  we are all people  of this world thank you so much  [Applause]

